erms of m). We construct universal words that suﬃce to hit all of P m as their weights vary, and we\n\nemploy classical quadrature schemes (from the theory of integration) to give eﬃcient words that induce the\n\nuniform measure on permutations. Finally, we study the dimension of P m and prove an upper bound for it\n\nin Theorem 5.12, also verifying computationally that the upper bound is an equality for m≤7.\n\n2\n\n1.2 A motivation for generalizing MST\n\nSome of the original motivation for this study came from shifted-interval MST because it is already in practi-\n\ncal use in a popular graph algorithm. Random spanning trees are a key ingredient in so-called recombination\n\nalgorithms that step from one random graph partition to another [7]. In the running of recombination\n\nalgorithms, two random parts of a graph partition are fused, and then a random spanning tree is used in\n\na bipartition step to divide them in a new way. A principal application is for generating random political\n\nhorizontal and vertical notation for permutations, as needed for clean exposition. We have seen that P m is\n\na semi-algebraic set in [0,1]m!. In this ﬁnal section, we ask the most basic question about P m: what is its\n\ndimension at generic points? (Fuller deﬁnitions and proofs for this section appear in Appendix E.)\n\nFor instance, P 3 sits in ∆(S 3), a simplex on 6 extreme points, so the most naive upper bound on its\n\ndimension is 5. This turns out to be tight: while P 3 does not contain every point in this simplex, it is still\n\nfull-dimensional. However, that is no longer true for P 4. This is due to additional constraints that hold of all\n\ndistributions in P 4, such as the independence constraints mentioned in Section 5.1. For instance, the event\n\nX 1< X 2 must be independent from the event X 3< X 4:\n\nP[X 1< X 2 and X 3< X 4] = P[X 1< X 2]·P[X 3< X 4].\n\nTranslating to permutation notation, the left-hand side has six terms, while the probabilities on the righ